  "html": "<div><section><div><section><p>This is the currently published alpha baseline and remains subject to final professional legal review.</p><div><blockquote><strong>INTERIM PUBLIC ALPHA BASELINE.</strong> This disclosure reflects the current reviewed architecture and may be updated as providers and deployment details change.</blockquote><p><strong>Effective date:</strong> August 17, 2026<br><strong>Last updated:</strong> November 20, 2026<br><strong>Document version:</strong> 0.5-public-alpha</p><p>This disclosure supplements the Tenvor Privacy Policy. Tenvor uses contracted service providers and independently operated compute nodes to deliver the Service.</p><h2 id=\"1-contracted-provider-categories\">1. Contracted provider categories</h2><div><div class=\"s-tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provider or category</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Data that may be processed</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clerk, Inc.</td><td>Authentication, session management, account protection</td><td>Account identifiers, sign-in attributes, session, device, and security data</td><td>Active authentication provider; Clerk’s own privacy and subprocessor disclosures also apply</td></tr><tr><td>Cloudflare infrastructure used by or through Clerk</td><td>Authentication challenges, bot and abuse protection</td><td>IP address, browser/device, request, and challenge data</td><td>Used for necessary security functions, not Tenvor behavioral advertising</td></tr><tr><td>E2B (FoundryLabs, Inc.)</td><td>Cloud sandbox that runs commands for Chat &amp; Work</td><td>Commands written by the model, the files selected for the run, command output, files the command creates, and an identifier for each command made by hashing the account ID with the command’s ID</td><td>A new sandbox is created for each command. Tenvor shuts it down afterwards, and it has a time limit of at most 60 seconds. Tenvor configures it to block outbound internet access unless the user turns that on for the run. Used under E2B’s standard terms and privacy policy</td></tr><tr><td>Exa (Exa Labs Inc.)</td><td>Web search for Chat &amp; Work</td><td>The search query written by the model, and request metadata from Tenvor’s server</td><td>Tenvor’s software sends no account identifier and no user IP address. Exa’s privacy policy said on October 2, 2026 that query data is used to improve its products, including model training. Used under Exa’s standard terms and privacy policy</td></tr><tr><td>Tenvor-operated or contracted hosting infrastructure</td><td>Public site, orchestrator, Chat &amp; Work service, database, storage, networking, and monitoring</td><td>Account, request, output, Chat &amp; Work conversation and generated-file, usage, security, ledger, node, and payout data depending on configuration</td><td>Primarily operated from the United States during alpha; current provider details are available on request at <a href=\"mailto:privacy@tenvor.io\">privacy@tenvor.io</a></td></tr><tr><td>GitHub, Inc.</td><td>Release distribution, repository-hosted documentation, and external links</td><td>IP, browser, download, account, and repository interaction data collected by GitHub</td><td>GitHub is not used as the private customer-content execution path</td></tr><tr><td>Email and support providers</td><td>Support, legal, privacy, security, abuse, and billing communications</td><td>Contact data, message content, attachments, and case records</td><td>Exact provider depends on the email service connected to <code>@tenvor.io</code></td></tr><tr><td>Authorize.net (a Visa solution)</td><td>Card payment processing, the hosted payment form, and customer and payment profiles for Plans and card purchases of Service Credits, when card payments are available</td><td>Card number, expiry, security code, and billing details are entered on Authorize.net’s form and are not received by Tenvor. Tenvor receives profile IDs, card brand, last four digits, expiry month and year, and transaction results</td><td>Used under Authorize.net’s terms and privacy statement. See Privacy Policy section 2.11</td></tr><tr><td>PaymentCloud, and the acquiring bank that holds Tenvor’s merchant account</td><td>Merchant account, settlement, and chargeback handling, when card payments are available</td><td>Transaction amount, date, card brand, last four digits, settlement and dispute records, and Tenvor’s business details</td><td>Used under Tenvor’s merchant account agreement and the provider’s own privacy policy. See Privacy Policy section 6.10</td></tr><tr><td>Hosted payout, identity, tax, or sanctions provider, when enabled</td><td>Provider onboarding, verification, payouts, and tax reporting</td><td>Legal name, address, country, date of birth, tax ID, tax forms, bank or payout details, identity documents, and verification results</td><td>Tenvor intends to use provider-hosted collection so full SSNs and identity documents do not need to pass through Tenvor’s application</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><p>Chat &amp; Work can also retrieve public web pages at the model’s request. Those requests go from Tenvor’s server to the website named in an address the model wrote. Websites are not Tenvor’s subprocessors. The website, and any site it redirects to, receives the address and Tenvor’s server address.</p><p>E2B, Exa, Authorize.net, and PaymentCloud are not bound by the Compute Provider Terms or the Provider Data Processing Schedule. Tenvor does not receive payment from these providers for this data and does not send it to them for advertising. The Privacy Policy, sections 6.9 and 6.10, describe these disclosures.</p><h2 id=\"2-independent-compute-providers\">2. Independent compute providers</h2><p>Tenvor’s core service uses independently operated machines rather than a single named data-center vendor. These providers are dynamic and may change from request to request.</p><p>A compute provider may receive the prompt, messages, conversation context, tool and schema content, model and generation settings, job identifiers, and output limits needed to perform an assignment. The provider should not receive the buyer’s Clerk identity, email, raw API key, full account ledger, or unrelated account data.</p><ul><li>Standard execution normally uses one eligible node.</li><li>Verified execution may use two nodes.</li><li>Audited execution may use a later shadow node.</li><li>Retry or failover may use another node.</li></ul><p>Compute providers are required by the Compute Provider Terms to process content only for the assignment, avoid human inspection, prohibit secondary use or training, maintain isolation, report incidents, and delete temporary content after the assignment. Tenvor cannot guarantee that independently operated hardware presents the same risk profile as a single-vendor data center.</p><p>Node participation is invitation-only or approval-based during the alpha. Tenvor may approve participation only where it reasonably believes operation is lawful and supportable. Tenvor does not currently promise customer-selected geographic routing or data residency.</p><h2 id=\"3-changes-and-questions\">3. Changes and questions</h2><p>Tenvor may add, remove, or replace providers as the alpha changes. Material changes that significantly affect personal-data handling will be reflected in the Privacy Policy or this disclosure.</p><p>Questions may be sent to <a href=\"mailto:privacy@tenvor.io\">privacy@tenvor.io</a>.</p><h2 id=\"document-changes\">Changes</h2><p>Version 0.3 (October 20, 2026) adds Authorize.net and PaymentCloud, which process card payments once Tenvor opens them. Version 0.4 (November 3, 2026) removes the cryptocurrency network provider, because Tenvor no longer funds accounts or pays operators in cryptocurrency.</p></div></section></div></section></div>"
